    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p11-q18-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the two-digit number that increases by $20\\%$ when its digits are reversed.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$45$.\n\nWriting the digits as $A$ and $B$ gives $4B=5A$, so $A=4$ and $B=5$.",
          "worked": "Let $A$ be the tens digit and $B$ the units digit, with $1\\le A\\le9$ and $0\\le B\\le9$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}10B+A&=1.2(10A+B)\\\\100B+10A&=12(10A+B)\\\\100B+10A&=120A+12B\\\\88B&=110A\\\\4B&=5A\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $A$ is a multiple of $4$, it is $4$ or $8$. The value $A=8$ would require $B=10$, which is not a digit. Hence $A=4$ and $B=5$, giving $45$.\n\nCheck: $1.2\\times45=54$, the reversed number.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 76
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q18-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that no two-digit number increases by $30\\%$ when its digits are reversed.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No such number exists.\n\nThe digit equation $29B=40A$ requires $A$ to be a multiple of $29$, contradicting $1\\le A\\le9$.",
          "worked": "Let $A$ be the tens digit and $B$ the units digit, with $1\\le A\\le9$ and $0\\le B\\le9$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}10B+A&=1.3(10A+B)\\\\100B+10A&=13(10A+B)\\\\100B+10A&=130A+13B\\\\87B&=120A\\\\29B&=40A\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $29$ and $40$ have no common factor, $A$ must be a multiple of $29$. This is impossible for a tens digit between $1$ and $9$. Therefore no such two-digit number exists.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 76
      }
    ]
  },
